Each mutant is a separate cp -a copy with no oracle probe; the failing test
is a COMMITTED test in internal/services/insights/trends_test.go.

(a) trends.go:44, PIN path reverted to master's form
    `totals[def.Name] -= t.Amount` -> `totals[def.Name] += math.Abs(t.Amount)`
    (keyword path at :49 left fixed, so only the pin path is mutated)
  $ cd cb3v2/m1_pin && go test -count=1 ./internal/services/insights/
      --- FAIL: TestAnalyzeMajorExpenseTrends_PinnedRefundNetsSigned
          trends_test.go:777: pinned CurrentAmount = 580.00, want 320
          (signed net via PIN path); abs gives 580
      RC=1.  SURVIVOR KILLED.
    I read the test (trends_test.go:746-779): the def's only keyword is
    "zzz-nomatch-2", which matches NEITHER description, so both rows can only
    reach the def through models.ResolveByIdentity(pins, t) -- a broken pin
    path drops the trend entirely (t.Fatalf on the missing key), a mis-signed
    pin path yields 580. The name matches what it asserts.

(b) spentSoFar reverted to abs
    `spentSoFar := -currentMonthOutflows.SumAmount()`
    -> `spentSoFar := math.Abs(currentMonthOutflows.SumAmount())`
  $ cd cb3v2/m2_spent && go test -count=1 ./internal/services/insights/
      --- FAIL: TestCalculateSpendingVelocity_MonthProjectionIdentity
          trends_test.go:811: MonthProjection = -15010.40, want -16170.40
          (signedSpent -580.00 + DailyAverage -556.80 * DaysRemaining 28)
      RC=1.  SURVIVOR KILLED, and killed TODAY (2026-09-02: DaysRemaining=28
      is the live clock, so the assertion fired on today's calendar).
    Date construction read (trends_test.go:789-813): monthStart is built in
    time.Local (the timezone SpendingVelocity itself uses), d2 = now-1h
    clamped up to now if it would precede monthStart, d1 = d2-1h clamped to
    monthStart+1min. signedSpent is hardcoded -580 (the load-bearing pin);
    the DailyAverage*DaysRemaining term is read back from the result, so this
    test pins spentSoFar specifically -- dailyAvg/historicalDaily stay pinned
    by _RefundReducesDailyAverage (550) and _RefundDominantPeriodIsNegative
    (-550), both of which I re-ran green.
    Calendar robustness, simulated minute-by-minute over a full year
    (python3, 366*24*60 iterations, checking monthStart <= d1,d2 <= now):
    13 bad minutes, all exactly 00:00 on the 1st. Analytically the window is
    now in [monthStart, monthStart+60s), where the clamp puts d1 one minute
    AFTER now, dropping the -280 row from the month bucket and breaking the
    hardcoded -580. See FINDING 1: a 60-second-per-month flake window, not a
    correctness defect and not in play today.

